## 3. The `/etc` directory — configuration you must know

`/etc` is one of the most important directories for a Linux administrator. It contains host-specific configuration for the operating system and installed services.

### Critical `/etc` files

/etc/fstab

Persistent filesystem mount definitions

/etc/hosts

Local hostname-to-IP mappings

/etc/hostname

Configured system hostname on typical RHEL installations

/etc/resolv.conf

DNS resolver configuration; may be generated or managed

/etc/passwd

User account records; passwords are not stored here

/etc/shadow

Password hashes and password-aging information; restricted access

/etc/group

Group definitions

/etc/gshadow

Restricted group security information

/etc/sudoers

Sudo policy; edit using visudo

/etc/sudoers.d/

Additional sudo policy files

/etc/ssh/sshd_config

SSH server configuration

/etc/ssh/ssh_config

SSH client configuration

/etc/hosts.allow

Legacy TCP Wrappers configuration; not a general modern RHEL firewall control

/etc/chrony.conf

Chrony time-synchronization configuration

/etc/rsyslog.conf

Main rsyslog configuration, if rsyslog is installed

/etc/logrotate.conf

Main log rotation configuration

/etc/logrotate.d/

Application-specific log rotation rules

/etc/security/limits.conf

PAM limits configuration; drop-ins may also apply

/etc/sysctl.conf

Sysctl settings; drop-ins under /etc/sysctl.d/ may also apply

/etc/selinux/config

Persistent SELinux mode and policy configuration

/etc/NetworkManager/

NetworkManager connection and daemon configuration

/etc/cron.d/

System cron schedules

/etc/crontab

System-wide cron table

/etc/systemd/system/

Administrator-defined systemd units and overrides

### Essential commands for `/etc`

```
# Inspect a configuration file
less /etc/fstab

# Show file permissions and ownership
ls -l /etc/ssh/sshd_config

# Find the configuration that systemd actually loads
systemctl cat sshd

# Check whether a file belongs to an installed RPM
rpm -qf /etc/ssh/sshd_config

# Find configuration files containing a keyword
grep -Rni "keyword" /etc/ssh/
```

The `grep` command above is an example; replace `keyword` with a real search term. Recursive searches can encounter generated files, permission errors, and sensitive configuration, so restrict searches to the relevant directories.

### Production change procedure

Before editing any configuration file:

1. Confirm the correct server, environment, and change ticket.
2. Read the existing configuration and identify its owner.
3. Back up the file, preserving permissions and ownership where needed.
4. Validate the new configuration using the service's supported test command.
5. Apply the change during the approved window.
6. Reload or restart only the affected service when required.
7. Verify the service, logs, and application behavior.
8. Keep a tested rollback procedure.

For example, before changing SSH server configuration:

```
sudo sshd -t
sudo systemctl status sshd
```

`sshd -t` checks SSH daemon configuration syntax. It does not prove network reachability or guarantee successful authentication. After an approved change, validate from a separate session before closing your existing administrative connection.

Never blindly overwrite `/etc/fstab`, `/etc/shadow`, `/etc/sudoers`, network configuration, or SSH configuration on a production server.

## 4. The `/var` directory — logs, application data and disk-full incidents

When an application fails, logs stop being written, or a server runs out of disk space, `/var` is a primary place to investigate.

| Path                | What to remember                                                     | Common investigation                 |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `/var/log/`         | System and application logs                                          | Identify errors and failures         |
| `/var/log/messages` | General system messages on systems using traditional rsyslog logging | Inspect system events                |
| `/var/log/secure`   | Authentication and security events on typical RHEL configurations    | Investigate login failures           |
| `/var/log/audit/`   | Audit logs when auditd is configured                                 | Investigate audited events           |
| `/var/log/cron`     | Cron-related logs when configured                                    | Investigate scheduled jobs           |
| `/var/cache/`       | Re-creatable application caches in many cases                        | Check cache growth                   |
| `/var/lib/`         | Persistent application and service state                             | Identify service data before cleanup |
| `/var/spool/`       | Queued work such as mail or print jobs                               | Investigate queue buildup            |
| `/var/tmp/`         | Longer-lived temporary files                                         | Investigate temporary storage growth |
| `/var/crash/`       | Crash dumps if configured                                            | Investigate system crashes           |
| `/var/www/`         | Common web content location, depending on configuration              | Inspect web content and ownership    |
| `/var/lib/rpm/`     | RPM database-related storage varies by implementation                | Package database diagnostics         |

These paths are common conventions, not guarantees. Logging may be handled by `systemd-journald`, rsyslog, an application-specific logger, or a centralized logging service. Check what your server actually uses.&#x20;

