### Production commands for `/var`

```
# Check filesystem space
df -hT

# Check inode usage
df -ih

# Check size of immediate directories on the root filesystem
sudo du -xhd1 /var 2>/dev/null

# List the largest items in /var, one filesystem at a time
sudo du -xah /var 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20

# View recent system journal entries
sudo journalctl -p warning -n 100 --no-pager

# View the current boot's errors
sudo journalctl -b -p err --no-pager

# Check a service's recent logs
sudo journalctl -u sshd -n 100 --no-pager
```

`-x` keeps `du` from crossing filesystem boundaries. The recursive size commands can be I/O-intensive on large systems, so use them thoughtfully during peak production periods.

### The disk-full problem: an important distinction

Disk blocks exhausted

`df -hT` reports a filesystem at or near 100% capacity. Large logs, application data, backups or deleted-but-open files may be responsible.

Inodes exhausted

`df -ih` reports 100% inode usage. A very large number of small files can exhaust inodes even when disk space remains.

Deleted files still consume space

A process may still hold an open file after it has been unlinked. `df` can report more usage than `du` accounts for.

To investigate deleted-but-open files:

```
sudo lsof +L1
```

If the command is available, inspect the process and file details. Do not kill processes or restart production services solely to reclaim space without understanding the impact and obtaining the required approval.

Golden rule: Never run `rm -rf /var/log/*` or delete files under `/var/lib` simply to free space. Use the correct log rotation, retention, queue-management or application-specific cleanup process.

## 5. The `/usr` directory — programs, binaries and libraries

The easiest way to remember `/usr` is: installed software and its supporting resources.

| Directory         | Purpose                                              | Useful command                      |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `/usr/bin`        | Most user commands                                   | `command -v ls`                     |
| `/usr/sbin`       | System administration programs                       | `command -v useradd`                |
| `/usr/lib`        | Libraries and supporting files                       | `find /usr/lib -maxdepth 1 -type d` |
| `/usr/lib64`      | Common 64-bit library location on RHEL               | `ls /usr/lib64`                     |
| `/usr/libexec`    | Helper programs used by other programs               | `ls /usr/libexec`                   |
| `/usr/share`      | Architecture-independent resources and documentation | `ls /usr/share`                     |
| `/usr/local/bin`  | Locally installed commands                           | `ls /usr/local/bin`                 |
| `/usr/local/sbin` | Locally installed administrative commands            | `ls /usr/local/sbin`                |
| `/usr/src`        | Source code and build-related files when installed   | `ls /usr/src`                       |

### How to locate a command

Suppose you need to find the executable for `systemctl`.

```
command -v systemctl
type -a systemctl
rpm -qf "$(command -v systemctl)"
```

What each command tells you:

- `command -v` — resolves the command using your shell's command lookup.
- `type -a` — shows matching aliases, functions or executable locations.
- `rpm -qf` — identifies the installed RPM package owning the resolved file, when applicable.

For a shared-library problem:

```
ldd /path/to/program
```

Only use `ldd` on trusted executables. For an unfamiliar or untrusted executable, use safer static inspection such as `readelf -d /path/to/program` where applicable.

### Production rule

Avoid manually replacing or deleting operating-system files under `/usr/bin`, `/usr/sbin`, `/usr/lib64` or `/lib64`. Use the RHEL package manager and approved patching procedures. Changes to libraries or essential commands can affect multiple services or prevent the system from booting.

